message.content:
Today is October 2026. I'm checking novelty before building a speculative-decoding method. Observation from my measurements (vLLM 0.30, AMD MI250, ROCm, TP=2, continuous batching, Spec-Bench): on HYBRID targets that mix Gated DeltaNet (linear-attention / recurrent) layers with full attention (Qwen3.8-27B: 48 Gated DeltaNet + 16 attention layers; Qwen3.6-35B-A3B hybrid MoE), the cost of verifying draft tokens under load is far higher than on attention-only targets of similar size (Gemma 4 31B, Gemma 4 26B-A4B). At 128 concurrent requests, going from 1 to 2 tokens per request adds +280 ms per step on Qwen3.8-27B vs +110 ms on Gemma 4 31B, and each additional verified token costs 101 vs 76 ms (MoE pair: 60 vs 16 ms/token), even though the hybrid's plain decode step is cheaper. Likely causes: per-draft-token recurrent state checkpointing for rollback (each GDN layer state is large: heads x d_k x d_v), token-sequential recurrent kernels in the verify path, or ROCm Triton kernel inefficiency. Separately, vLLM's model-free n-gram/suffix proposers produced wrong outputs on these hybrid models (recurrent state apparently not rolled back after rejections).

Search arXiv, OpenReview, GitHub (vLLM, SGLang, fla-org/flash-linear-attention, Qwen, NVIDIA Nemotron-H, AI21 Jamba, Mamba/state-space repos), and blogs thoroughly for prior or concurrent work (2023–Oct 2026) on:
1. the cost of speculative-decoding VERIFICATION for state-space / linear-attention / hybrid models (Mamba, Mamba-2, Gated DeltaNet, RWKV, Jamba, Nemotron-H, Qwen3-Next/3.5+, Kimi Linear, MiniMax), including state rollback / checkpointing strategies (store every intermediate state vs recompute from a checkpoint vs "lazy commit"), chunked/parallel-scan verification of k tokens, tree verification over recurrent layers (e.g. STree, "Snakes and Ladders", "When Linear Attention Meets Autoregressive Decoding", SpecMamba, any 2026 work);
2. systems work that measured speculative decoding speedup collapsing on hybrid models under batching, or that proposes hybrid-aware draft-length / verification policies;
3. vLLM / SGLang implementation details for Mamba/GDN speculative decoding: how intermediate states are stored (e.g. num_speculative_blocks, per-token state indices, "mamba_cache_mode", prefix caching for hybrids), known performance issues/PRs/issues (especially on ROCm), and any fixes merged in 2026.

For each relevant item: title, arXiv id + month or PR/issue number + date, venue if any, what exactly it does/measures, and how close it is to (a) diagnosing hybrid verification cost under load, (b) a method that reduces it (e.g. checkpoint-free / recompute-based rollback, verifying recurrent layers with a chunked parallel form, hybrid-aware speculation policy). End with a 3-sentence verdict on what remains novel. Be specific and concise; do not write files.

